 2. Post-midterms sentiment surge 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: University of Michigan; Chart: Neil Irwin/Axios Consumer sentiment has popped this month, amid midterm elections and a moderation in energy prices.

 The big picture: Sentiment remains below its pre-Iran War levels, but Americans felt better about their personal finances this month — and view the soon-to-be divided government as better for their pocketbooks.

 Expectations surged for independents and Democrats but fell for Republicans, which supports the idea that sentiment was influenced by respondents' view of the midterm election results. By the numbers: The University of Michigan's overall consumer sentiment index was 51.5 in November, up from 48 in October. That 7.3% rise was fueled by a particularly strong gain in expectations for the future (up 8.4%).

 Median expectations for inflation over the next year plunged to 4.7% from 3.7% in October, and longer-run inflation expectations dropped as well, to 3.3% from 3.5%. What they're saying: "Consumers appear to anticipate potential changes to public policy under a divided government to come," wrote survey director Joanne Hsu.

 Meanwhile, personal financial conditions improved for low-wealth and middle-wealth consumers, the latter of which "experienced some relief" with gas prices falling. "In contrast, those with the largest stockholdings reported a decline in personal finances, consistent with continued AI-related agitation in stock markets," Hsu notes. Reality check: Sentiment remains 9% below its February level, before the Iran conflict, and consumers' inflation expectations remain above their 2024 range.

 Hsu wrote that "consumers continue to report overwhelmingly that inflation — rather than unemployment — will cause greater hardship" in the next year or two. 
 
